{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NffvEZ5hygUwBdfQT7UfuG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/packet_loop.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Bundle of types and entry points implementing quic's\n4\t//! reference event loop (`sockloop.c`) on top of the [`socks`]\n5\t//! UDP shims.  The loop:\n6\t//!\n7\t//! * owns one or more [`SocketCtx`] per network family,\n8\t//! * polls them with [`socks::select`] (or `select`/\n9\t//!   `WSAWaitForMultipleEvents` in the C original),\n10\t//! * routes received datagrams into quic, and\n11\t//! * fires application callbacks ([`PacketLoopCbFn`]) at\n12\t//!   well-defined points (loop ready, after recv, after send,\n13\t//!   address change, time check, system-call duration spike, wake-up,\n14\t//!   alt-port).\n15\t//!\n16\t//! Three layered entry points cover the same loop with different\n17\t//! parameter shapes:\n18\t//!\n19\t//! * [`run`] — the original 1.x signature, preserved for\n20\t//!   compatibility.  Builds a [`LoopParam`] on the stack and forwards.\n21\t//! * [`run_v2`] — takes a [`LoopParam`] directly.\n22\t//! * [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] — takes a fully-populated\n23\t//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`]; the threaded entry point.\n24\t//!\n25\t//! Plus the threading helpers ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`] et al.)\n26\t//! that wrap `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n27\t//!\n28\t//! Phase 4: threading entry points (`run_v2`, `run`, spawn family,\n29\t//! `open_sockets`) are `unimplemented!()` — threading is deferred to\n30\t//! v2 per TRANSLATE_PLAN.md.  Leaf helpers are implemented.\n31\t//!\n32\t//! [`socks`]: crate::socks\n33\t//!\n34\t//! ## Pointer-shape decisions\n35\t//!\n36\t//! Read from `quic/sockloop.c`, `quic/winsockloop.c`, and\n37\t//! the demo callers in `first/demo.c`,\n38\t//! `sample/sample_*.c`, and `test/sockloop_test.c`:\n39\t//!\n40\t//! * `Quic*` parameters are non-null in every observed\n41\t//!   caller — they translate to `&mut Quic`.\n42\t//! * `LoopParam*` is *borrowed* by the loop\n43\t//!   helpers (the caller stack-allocates it in\n44\t//!   `run_v2` / the demo apps) — `&mut` here.  Inside\n45\t//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`] the same pointer is *owned*\n46\t//!   when `is_param_allocated == 1` ([`Config::start_server_threads`]\n47\t//!   `malloc`s a copy and `delete_network_thread` `free`s\n48\t//!   it).  Phase 1 keeps the field as `Option<Box<…>>` and folds\n49\t//!   the C `is_param_allocated` flag away — the `Box` itself\n50\t//!   carries ownership; borrowed-param call sites store `None`\n51\t//!   plus a separate borrow at runtime.\n52\t//! * `picoquic_quic_config_t*` is borrowed-mut by\n53\t//!   [`Quic::create_server`] and [`Config::start_server_threads`]\n54\t//!   (they read and may mutate fields like `ticket_encryption_key`).\n55\t//! * Function-pointer typedefs (`packet_loop_cb_fn`,\n56\t//!   `custom_thread_create_fn`, `_setname_fn`,\n57\t//!   `_delete_fn`) become traits per the Phase 1 rules; the C `void*\n58\t//!   callback_ctx` companion folds into the trait implementor's\n59\t//!   state.  The C `(cb_mode, void* callback_argv)` tagged-union\n60\t//!   pair becomes the typed [`LoopEvent`] enum — each variant\n61\t//!   carries the payload type the C `cb_mode` implied.\n62\t//! * `SocketCtx* s_ctx` is used as both a single object\n63\t//!   ([`SocketCtx::close`]) and a fixed-size array\n64\t//!   ([`open_sockets`] writes up to `PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`\n65\t//!   entries).  The single-object path is a method on [`SocketCtx`];\n66\t//!   the array path takes `&mut [SocketCtx]`, with the slice length\n67\t//!   subsuming the C convention of \"callee writes and returns the count\".\n68\t//! * `NetworkThreadCtx**` outputs from\n69\t//!   [`Config::start_server_threads`] become `&mut [Option<Box<…>>]`\n70\t//!   — the slice carries `nb_threads_max`; each slot is filled with\n71\t//!   `Some` on success.\n72\t//! * Bitfields collapse to `bool`s.  Single-bit C `int : 1` /\n73\t//!   `unsigned int : 1` flag fields don't justify `bitflags!` on a\n74\t//!   set of unrelated booleans (see `config.rs` for the\n75\t//!   established convention).\n76\t//! * `volatile int` fields in [`NetworkThreadCtx`]\n77\t//!   become plain `i32` / `bool` — `Send`/`Sync` is out of v1 scope,\n78\t//!   so the volatile semantics have nowhere to land.\n79\t//! * `sockaddr_storage` fields fold into `Option<SocketAddr>`,\n80\t//!   matching the [`socks`] convention (`AF_UNSPEC` ↔ `None`).\n81\t//! * The Windows-only fields (`overlap`, `WSARecvMsg`, `WSASendMsg`,\n82\t//!   `dataBuf`, `msg`, …) and the io_uring fields are dropped per\n83\t//!   the v1 single-target scope.\n84\t//! * `recv_buffer: uint8_t* + recv_buffer_size: size_t` is an\n85\t//!   owning pair (allocated in `packet_set_windows_socket`\n86\t//!   on the Windows path, or `packet_loop_recv_buf_uring_init`\n87\t//!   on io_uring; freed in [`SocketCtx::close`]).\n88\t//!   On the canonical Linux/`select` build neither is used — every\n89\t//!   datagram lands in the shared loop buffer.  The field shape is\n90\t//!   `Option<Vec<u8>>` so the unused state is `None`; Vec subsumes\n91\t//!   the (pointer, size) pair into one owning value.\n92\t\n93\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n94\t\n95\tuse std::thread::JoinHandle;\n96\t\n97\tuse crate::Error;\n98\tuse crate::Instant;\n99\tuse crate::config::Config;\n100\tuse crate::socks::OsError;\n101\tuse crate::{AlpnSelect, Quic, StreamDataCallback};\n102\t\n103\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n104\t// Compile-time limits.\n105\t\n106\t/// Maximum number of UDP sockets a single packet loop can drive at\n107\t/// once (one per address family, optionally times two for the\n108\t/// public/private port split).  C: `PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`.\n109\tpub const PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX: usize = 4;\n110\t\n111\t/// Maximum datagrams pulled from one socket per loop iteration.\n112\t/// C: `PACKET_LOOP_RECV_MAX`.\n113\tpub const PACKET_LOOP_RECV_MAX: usize = 10;\n114\t\n115\t/// Maximum datagrams pushed to one socket per loop iteration.\n116\t/// C: `PACKET_LOOP_SEND_MAX`.\n117\tpub const PACKET_LOOP_SEND_MAX: usize = 10;\n118\t\n119\t/// Hard cap on how long the loop will wait between iterations\n120\t/// (microseconds).  C: `PACKET_LOOP_SEND_DELAY_MAX`.\n121\tpub const PACKET_LOOP_SEND_DELAY_MAX: u64 = 2500;\n122\t\n123\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n124\t// Per-socket context.\n125\t\n126\t/// State the loop maintains per UDP socket.  C: `socket_ctx_t`.\n127\t///\n128\t/// Trimmed to the canonical Linux build: the Windows\n129\t/// (`WSAOVERLAPPED`, `WSARecvMsg`, …) and io_uring (`msghdr`,\n130\t/// `iovec`, `ctrl_buffer`) fields are dropped per the v1\n131\t/// single-target scope.  The four C bitfields collapse to plain\n132\t/// `bool` flags.\n133\tpub struct SocketCtx<S: crate::socks::Socket> {\n134\t    /// OS socket handle.  `None` mirrors the C `INVALID_SOCKET`\n135\t    /// sentinel for \"not yet open\".  Generic over the active\n136\t    /// `Socket` implementation; the default in production is\n137\t    /// `crate::socks_socket2::Socket2Udp` (wraps `socket2::Socket`).\n138\t    pub fd: Option<S>,\n139\t    /// Address family the socket was opened in (`AF_INET`,\n140\t    /// `AF_INET6`).  Stays `i32` to match call sites that pass the\n141\t    /// libc `AF_*` constants directly.\n142\t    pub af: i32,\n143\t    /// Port number the socket is bound to (host byte order).\n144\t    pub port: u16,\n145\t    /// `htons(port)` cached so the loop can stamp it into received\n146\t    /// destination addresses without a per-packet byte-swap.\n147\t    pub n_port: u16,\n148\t    /// Private port number — the port reserved for this thread when\n149\t    /// the public port is shared with siblings (see\n150\t    /// `is_port_shared`).  C: `private_port`.\n151\t    pub private_port: u16,\n152\t    /// Whether this socket is bound to a port shared with other\n153\t    /// threads (e.g., port 443 across an H3 thread pool — uses\n154\t    /// `SO_REUSEPORT`).  C: `int is_port_shared : 1`.\n155\t    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n156\t    /// Whether [`open_sockets`]-equivalent setup has completed\n157\t    /// for this socket.  C: `unsigned int is_started : 1`.\n158\t    pub is_started: bool,\n159\t    /// Whether the kernel honored `UDP_SEGMENT` (GSO) on this\n160\t    /// socket.  C: `unsigned int supports_udp_send_coalesced : 1`.\n161\t    pub supports_udp_send_coalesced: bool,\n162\t    /// Whether the kernel honored `UDP_GRO` on this socket.\n163\t    /// C: `unsigned int supports_udp_recv_coalesced : 1`.\n164\t    pub supports_udp_recv_coalesced: bool,\n165\t    /// Owning receive buffer.  Used only on the Windows-async and\n166\t    /// io_uring paths; the canonical Linux build leaves it as\n167\t    /// `None` and uses the shared loop buffer instead.\n168\t    /// C: `uint8_t* recv_buffer` plus the `recv_buffer_size`\n169\t    /// companion (length is implicit in the slice).\n170\t    pub recv_buffer: Option<Box<[u8]>>,\n171\t    /// Source address of the most recent datagram (`recvmsg`'s\n172\t    /// `msg_name`).  C: `struct sockaddr_storage addr_from` plus\n173\t    /// `from_length` — `Option<SocketAddr>` carries both.\n174\t    pub addr_from: Option<SocketAddr>,\n175\t    /// Destination address parsed out of `IP_PKTINFO` /\n176\t    /// `IPV6_PKTINFO` for the most recent datagram.\n177\t    /// C: `struct sockaddr_storage addr_dest` + `dest_length`.\n178\t    pub addr_dest: Option<SocketAddr>,\n179\t    /// Receiving interface index (from the same cmsg).\n180\t    /// C: `int dest_if`.\n181\t    pub dest_if: i32,\n182\t    /// `IP_TOS` / `IPV6_TCLASS` ECN code-point on the most recent\n183\t    /// datagram.  C: `unsigned char received_ecn`.\n184\t    pub received_ecn: u8,\n185\t    /// Number of bytes read into the loop buffer on the most recent\n186\t    /// recv.  C: `int bytes_recv` (C uses `-1` for error; the Rust\n187\t    /// loop propagates errors through `Result` at the call site and\n188\t    /// only writes a valid byte count here).\n189\t    pub bytes_recv: usize,\n190\t    /// Scratch space for assembling outbound `cmsg` payloads\n191\t    /// (`IP_PKTINFO`, `IPV6_PKTINFO`, `UDP_SEGMENT`).\n192\t    /// C: `char cmsg_buffer[1024]`.\n193\t    pub cmsg_buffer: [u8; 1024],\n194\t    /// `UDP_GRO` segment size on the most recent datagram (the\n195\t    /// kernel's `UDP_GRO` cmsg).  C: `size_t udp_coalesced_size`.\n196\t    pub udp_coalesced_size: usize,\n197\t}\n198\t\n199\timpl<S: crate::socks::Socket> Default for SocketCtx<S> {\n200\t    fn default() -> Self {\n201\t        SocketCtx {\n202\t            fd: None,\n203\t            af: 0,\n204\t            port: 0,\n205\t            n_port: 0,\n206\t            private_port: 0,\n207\t            is_port_shared: false,\n208\t            is_started: false,\n209\t            supports_udp_send_coalesced: false,\n210\t            supports_udp_recv_coalesced: false,\n211\t            recv_buffer: None,\n212\t            addr_from: None,\n213\t            addr_dest: None,\n214\t            dest_if: 0,\n215\t            received_ecn: 0,\n216\t            bytes_recv: 0,\n217\t            cmsg_buffer: [0; 1024],\n218\t            udp_coalesced_size: 0,\n219\t        }\n220\t    }\n221\t}\n222\t\n223\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n224\t// Callback enum + companion arg structs.\n225\t\n226\t/// Loop-callback event delivered to [`PacketLoopCbFn::callback`].\n227\t/// Replaces the C `(packet_loop_cb_enum cb_mode, void* callback_argv)`\n228\t/// pair: the enum carries the per-event payload directly, so\n229\t/// implementors no longer cast a `void*`.\n230\t/// C: `packet_loop_cb_enum`.\n231\t#[derive(Debug)]\n232\tpub enum LoopEvent<'a> {\n233\t    /// Loop has finished initializing.  C: `packet_loop_ready`,\n234\t    /// `callback_argv: *mut LoopOptions`.\n235\t    Ready(&'a mut LoopOptions),\n236\t    /// Number of packets received this iteration.  C:\n237\t    /// `packet_loop_after_receive`, `callback_argv: *mut size_t`.\n238\t    AfterReceive(usize),\n239\t    /// Number of packets sent this iteration.  C:\n240\t    /// `packet_loop_after_send`, `callback_argv: *mut size_t`.\n241\t    AfterSend(usize),\n242\t    /// New local address advertised by the application after a\n243\t    /// port update.  C: `packet_loop_port_update`,\n244\t    /// `callback_argv: *mut sockaddr`.\n245\t    PortUpdate(core::net::SocketAddr),\n246\t    /// Optional check-in fired when the application set\n247\t    /// [`LoopOptions::do_time_check`].  C:\n248\t    /// `packet_loop_time_check`.\n249\t    TimeCheck(&'a mut TimeCheckArg),\n250\t    /// Optional system-call duration report fired when the\n251\t    /// application set [`LoopOptions::do_system_call_duration`].\n252\t    /// C: `packet_loop_system_call_duration`.\n253\t    SystemCallDuration(&'a mut SystemCallDuration),\n254\t    /// Wake-up event triggered by [`NetworkThreadCtx::wake_up`].\n255\t    /// C: `packet_loop_wake_up` with `callback_argv: NULL`.\n256\t    WakeUp,\n257\t    /// Alt-port address surfaced for multipath / migration tests.\n258\t    /// C: `packet_loop_alt_port`.\n259\t    AltPort(core::net::SocketAddr),\n260\t}\n261\t\n262\t/// System-call duration statistics surfaced through the optional\n263\t/// [`LoopEvent::SystemCallDuration`] callback.\n264\t/// C: `packet_loop_system_call_duration_t`.\n265\t#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\n266\tpub struct SystemCallDuration {\n267\t    /// Duration of the most recent zero-delay system call\n268\t    /// (microseconds).\n269\t    pub scd_last: u64,\n270\t    /// Maximum observed system-call duration so far.\n271\t    pub scd_max: u64,\n272\t    /// Smoothed system-call duration (RFC-6298–style EWMA).\n273\t    pub scd_smoothed: u64,\n274\t    /// Mean absolute deviation around `scd_smoothed`.\n275\t    pub scd_dev: u64,\n276\t}\n277\t\n278\t/// In/out argument for the [`LoopEvent::TimeCheck`] callback.\n279\t/// The loop fills `current_time` and a proposed `delta_t`;\n280\t/// the application overwrites `delta_t` with a (possibly smaller)\n281\t/// value if it has work to do sooner.\n282\t/// C: `packet_loop_time_check_arg_t`.\n283\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]\n284\tpub struct TimeCheckArg {\n285\t    /// Loop-time timestamp.\n286\t    pub current_time: Instant,\n287\t    /// Proposed sleep, in microseconds — application may shrink.\n288\t    pub delta_t: i64,\n289\t}\n290\t\n291\timpl Default for TimeCheckArg {\n292\t    fn default() -> Self {\n293\t        Self {\n294\t            current_time: Instant::from_ticks(0),\n295\t            delta_t: 0,\n296\t        }\n297\t    }\n298\t}\n299\t\n300\t/// Application callback fired at the lifecycle and per-iteration\n301\t/// events listed in [`LoopEvent`].\n302\t///\n303\t/// C: `int (*packet_loop_cb_fn)(picoquic_quic_t* quic,\n304\t/// packet_loop_cb_enum cb_mode, void* callback_ctx, void*\n305\t/// callback_argv)`.  The `callback_ctx` companion folds into the\n306\t/// trait implementor's state; the `(cb_mode, callback_argv)` tagged\n307\t/// union folds into [`LoopEvent`].\n308\t///\n309\t/// Returns `Ok(())` on success, or an error to break the loop.\n310\tpub trait PacketLoopCbFn {\n311\t    fn callback(&mut self, quic: &mut Quic, event: LoopEvent<'_>) -> Result<(), Error>;\n312\t}\n313\t\n314\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n315\t// Loop options + parameters.\n316\t\n317\t/// Feature-flags the application advertises in response to the\n318\t/// [`LoopEvent::Ready`]\n319\t/// callback.  C: `packet_loop_options_t`, three single-bit\n320\t/// `unsigned int : 1` fields collapsed to `bool` per the\n321\t/// established convention (see `config.rs`).\n322\t#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\n323\tpub struct LoopOptions {\n324\t    /// Application wants the loop to call back with\n325\t    /// [`LoopEvent::TimeCheck`]\n326\t    /// before each select.\n327\t    pub do_time_check: bool,\n328\t    /// Application wants notifications when zero-delay system calls\n329\t    /// take noticeably long.\n330\t    pub do_system_call_duration: bool,\n331\t    /// Application provides an alternate port for migration /\n332\t    /// multipath testing.\n333\t    pub provide_alt_port: bool,\n334\t}\n335\t\n336\t/// Loop-construction parameters (v2 / v3 entry points).\n337\t/// C: `packet_loop_param_t`.  `repr(C)` is dropped — the\n338\t/// struct never crosses an external boundary.\n339\t#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\n340\tpub struct LoopParam {\n341\t    /// Default port for outgoing connections.  `0` ⇒ ephemeral.\n342\t    pub local_port: u16,\n343\t    /// `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6`, or `0` for \"both\".\n344\t    pub local_af: i32,\n345\t    /// Outbound interface index (or `0` to leave it to the kernel).\n346\t    pub dest_if: i32,\n347\t    /// Server / public-facing port.  `0` for client-only loops.\n348\t    pub public_port: u16,\n349\t    /// Whether the public port is shared across this thread pool\n350\t    /// (uses `SO_REUSEPORT`).\n351\t    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n352\t    /// `SO_SNDBUF` / `SO_RCVBUF` size, or `0` to skip the syscall.\n353\t    pub socket_buffer_size: i32,\n354\t    /// Disable `UDP_SEGMENT` GSO even if the kernel advertises it.\n355\t    pub do_not_use_gso: bool,\n356\t    /// Open the public-port socket pair too (needed for migration).\n357\t    pub extra_socket_required: bool,\n358\t    /// When both port pairs exist, prefer the extra socket as the\n359\t    /// outgoing source.\n360\t    pub prefer_extra_socket: bool,\n361\t    /// Inject a synthetic `EIO` once into the send path (debug\n362\t    /// hook).\n363\t    pub simulate_eio: bool,\n364\t    /// Cap on the size of an individual coalesced send.  `0` ⇒\n365\t    /// kernel default.\n366\t    pub send_length_max: usize,\n367\t}\n368\t\n369\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n370\t// Custom thread-management hooks.\n371\t\n372\t/// Spawn-a-thread hook.  C:\n373\t/// `int (*custom_thread_create_fn)(void** thread_id,\n374\t/// thread_fn thread_fn, void* arg)`.\n375\t///\n376\t/// In C, `(thread_fn, arg)` is the trampoline + state pair the new\n377\t/// thread will run.  In Rust, [`std::thread::spawn`] already takes\n378\t/// an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send` closure (which subsumes the C\n379\t/// \"function pointer + void* arg\" pattern), so the hook reduces to\n380\t/// \"produce a [`JoinHandle`] from that closure\".\n381\tpub trait CustomThreadCreateFn {\n382\t    /// Create a thread that runs `thread_fn` to completion.  The\n383\t    /// returned [`JoinHandle`] is owned by the caller and passed\n384\t    /// back through [`CustomThreadDeleteFn::delete`] at teardown.\n385\t    ///\n386\t    /// The C contract returns `0` for success / non-zero `errno`\n387\t    /// otherwise; the [`OsError`] payload carries the same value\n388\t    /// that the C `*ret` out-parameter would on\n389\t    /// [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`].\n390\t    fn create(\n391\t        &mut self,\n392\t        thread_fn: Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + 'static>,\n393\t    ) -> Result<JoinHandle<()>, OsError>;\n394\t}\n395\t\n396\t/// Set-thread-name hook.  C:\n397\t/// `void (*custom_thread_setname_fn)(char const* thread_name)`.\n398\t///\n399\t/// Length is capped at 16 bytes (including the trailing NUL) on\n400\t/// Linux's `prctl(PR_SET_NAME, …)`; the trait takes a `&str` slice\n401\t/// and lets the implementor truncate / re-encode as needed.\n402\tpub trait CustomThreadSetnameFn {\n403\t    /// Apply `thread_name` to the *current* thread.  Called from\n404\t    /// inside the thread after it starts, per the C convention.\n405\t    fn set_name(&mut self, thread_name: &str);\n406\t}\n407\t\n408\t/// Tear-down-a-thread hook.  C:\n409\t/// `void (*custom_thread_delete_fn)(void** thread_id)`.\n410\t///\n411\t/// In Rust the standard `JoinHandle` cleans itself up on drop, so\n412\t/// this hook only matters for backends that allocate their own\n413\t/// out-of-band thread bookkeeping; the default\n414\t/// [`internal_thread_delete`] just lets the handle go.\n415\tpub trait CustomThreadDeleteFn {\n416\t    /// Release any resources tied to `thread`.  Called from the\n417\t    /// destruction path of [`NetworkThreadCtx`].\n418\t    fn delete(&mut self, thread: JoinHandle<()>);\n419\t}\n420\t\n421\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n422\t// Threaded packet-loop context.\n423\t\n424\t/// Thread context carrying everything the v3 entry point needs to\n425\t/// run a packet loop.  C: `network_thread_ctx_t`.\n426\t///\n427\t/// Two ownership modes coexist in C:\n428\t///\n429\t/// * Foreground / blocking (`run_v2`): the caller stack-allocates a\n430\t///   `NetworkThreadCtx` and calls [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] on it.\n431\t///   `is_threaded` stays `false`, no wake-up plumbing is created.\n432\t/// * Background ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`]): the helper\n433\t///   allocates the context, populates it, opens the wake-up\n434\t///   pipe / event, and launches the OS thread; teardown via\n435\t///   `Drop` (Phase 3) frees everything.\n436\t///\n437\t/// Phase 1 keeps a single struct shape with optional fields so both\n438\t/// modes fit; Phase 3 may split them or introduce a builder.\n439\t///\n440\t/// `volatile` qualifiers in the C source are dropped — `Send`/`Sync`\n441\t/// is out of v1 scope and the thread-shutdown handshake uses these\n442\t/// flags through the same single-threaded code path the rest of the\n443\t/// crate assumes.\n444\tpub struct NetworkThreadCtx {\n445\t    // The C field `picoquic_quic_t* quic` is gone.  v1 is\n446\t    // single-threaded (per `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`), so the loop's\n447\t    // entry points (`Quic::run_loop` etc.) take `&mut Quic`\n448\t    // explicitly rather than threading a back-pointer through\n449\t    // this struct.  v2 will reintroduce a typed cross-thread\n450\t    // handle (`Arc<Mutex<Quic>>` or whatever the threading model\n451\t    // ends up using); the placeholder here is intentionally\n452\t    // absent.\n453\t    /// Loop parameters, optionally owned (`is_param_allocated`).\n454\t    /// Phase 1 collapses the C borrowed-or-owned discriminant into\n455\t    /// a single `Option<Box<…>>`; the borrowed case stores `None`\n456\t    /// and the borrow lives outside this struct.\n457\t    pub param: Option<Box<LoopParam>>,\n458\t    /// Application loop callback.  `None` matches the C `NULL`\n459\t    /// (the loop runs without notifying the app on each event —\n460\t    /// used by the bench harness).\n461\t    pub loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n462\t    /// Custom thread-deletion hook; defaults to\n463\t    /// `internal_thread_delete` when the C caller passes\n464\t    /// `NULL`.\n465\t    pub thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n466\t    /// Custom thread-naming hook; defaults to\n467\t    /// `internal_thread_setname`.\n468\t    pub thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n469\t    /// Optional thread name.  Capped at 16 bytes (including NUL)\n470\t    /// on Linux per `prctl(PR_SET_NAME, …)`.\n471\t    pub thread_name: Option<String>,\n472\t    /// Underlying OS thread handle.  `None` for the foreground v2\n473\t    /// entry path (no thread is spawned).\n474\t    pub pthread: Option<JoinHandle<()>>,\n475\t    /// Wake-up pipe (Linux: `pipe2`).  `[ -1, -1 ]` when no wake-up\n476\t    /// has been opened (`wake_up_defined == false`).  The Windows\n477\t    /// `HANDLE wake_up_event` variant is dropped per the v1 scope.\n478\t    pub wake_up_pipe_fd: [i32; 2],\n479\t    /// Whether [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`] actually\n480\t    /// spawned a thread (vs. the foreground path that reuses the\n481\t    /// caller's stack).  C: `int is_threaded`.\n482\t    pub is_threaded: bool,\n483\t    /// Whether [`Self::wake_up_pipe_fd`] was successfully opened.\n484\t    /// C: `int wake_up_defined`.\n485\t    pub wake_up_defined: bool,\n486\t    /// Set by the loop once it finishes initialization (after the\n487\t    /// first [`LoopEvent::Ready`] callback).\n488\t    /// C: `volatile int thread_is_ready`.\n489\t    pub thread_is_ready: bool,\n490\t    /// Set from the outside to ask the loop to exit (the `Drop`\n491\t    /// implementation will set this in Phase 3).\n492\t    /// C: `volatile int thread_should_close`.\n493\t    pub thread_should_close: bool,\n494\t    /// Set by the loop on the way out, before returning from v3.\n495\t    /// C: `volatile int thread_is_closed`.\n496\t    pub thread_is_closed: bool,\n497\t    /// Final return code from the loop body.  Read by the caller\n498\t    /// of [`run_v2`] after [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] returns.\n499\t    /// C: `int return_code`.\n500\t    pub return_code: i32,\n501\t}\n502\t\n503\timpl Default for NetworkThreadCtx {\n504\t    fn default() -> Self {\n505\t        NetworkThreadCtx {\n506\t            param: None,\n507\t            loop_callback: None,\n508\t            thread_delete_fn: None,\n509\t            thread_setname_fn: None,\n510\t            thread_name: None,\n511\t            pthread: None,\n512\t            wake_up_pipe_fd: [-1, -1],\n513\t            is_threaded: false,\n514\t            wake_up_defined: false,\n515\t            thread_is_ready: false,\n516\t            thread_should_close: false,\n517\t            thread_is_closed: false,\n518\t            return_code: 0,\n519\t        }\n520\t    }\n521\t}\n522\t\n523\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n524\t// Loop entry points.\n525\t\n526\timpl Quic {\n527\t    /// Drive the packet loop until the application or an error breaks\n528\t    /// it.  C: `int packet_loop_v2(…)`.\n529\t    ///\n530\t    /// Builds a transient [`NetworkThreadCtx`] on the stack and calls\n531\t    /// [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`].  The return code is what `run` stored\n532\t    /// in `thread_ctx.return_code`.\n533\t    pub fn run_v2(\n534\t        &mut self,\n535\t        _param: &mut LoopParam,\n536\t        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n537\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n538\t        unimplemented!()\n539\t    }\n540\t}\n541\t\n542\timpl Quic {\n543\t    /// Legacy single-call entry point; builds a [`LoopParam`] from\n544\t    /// positional arguments and forwards to [`run_v2`].\n545\t    /// C: `int packet_loop(…)`.\n546\t    pub fn run(\n547\t        &mut self,\n548\t        _local_port: i32,\n549\t        _local_af: i32,\n550\t        _dest_if: i32,\n551\t        _socket_buffer_size: i32,\n552\t        _do_not_use_gso: bool,\n553\t        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n554\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n555\t        unimplemented!()\n556\t    }\n557\t}\n558\t\n559\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n560\t// NetworkThreadCtx: background-thread management and the v3 entry point.\n561\t\n562\timpl NetworkThreadCtx {\n563\t    /// Run the packet loop using `self` as the fully-populated thread\n564\t    /// context.  C: `void* packet_loop_v3(void* v_ctx)`.\n565\t    ///\n566\t    /// The C entry returns its `void*` exit code only to satisfy the\n567\t    /// thread-function prototype; callers read the result back from\n568\t    /// `return_code`.\n569\t    pub fn run(&mut self) {\n570\t        unimplemented!()\n571\t    }\n572\t\n573\t    /// Spawn a packet loop on its own OS thread using the platform\n574\t    /// default (`pthread_create` / `CreateThread`).\n575\t    /// C: `network_thread_ctx_t* start_network_thread(…)`.\n576\t    ///\n577\t    /// Returns `Ok(Box<Self>)` on success — the box owns the\n578\t    /// heap-allocated context.  `Err(OsError)` carries the OS errno\n579\t    /// from the wake-up pipe or thread-create syscall (the C `*ret`\n580\t    /// out-parameter folds into the result).\n581\t    pub fn spawn(\n582\t        _quic: &mut Quic,\n583\t        _param: LoopParam,\n584\t        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n585\t    ) -> Result<Box<Self>, OsError> {\n586\t        unimplemented!()\n587\t    }\n588\t\n589\t    /// Spawn the packet loop using application-supplied thread hooks.\n590\t    /// `None` for any hook selects the platform default — same\n591\t    /// semantics as the C `NULL` argument.\n592\t    /// C: `network_thread_ctx_t* start_custom_network_thread(…)`.\n593\t    #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n594\t    pub fn spawn_custom(\n595\t        _quic: &mut Quic,\n596\t        _param: LoopParam,\n597\t        _thread_create_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadCreateFn>>,\n598\t        _thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n599\t        _thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n600\t        _thread_name: Option<&str>,\n601\t        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n602\t    ) -> Result<Box<Self>, OsError> {\n603\t        unimplemented!()\n604\t    }\n605\t\n606\t    /// Write to the wake-up pipe so the loop's next iteration runs\n607\t    /// immediately and fires [`LoopEvent::WakeUp`].\n608\t    /// C: `int wake_up_network_thread(network_thread_ctx_t*)`.\n609\t    ///\n610\t    /// Returns `Err(OsError)` with the OS errno on failure, `Ok(())`\n611\t    /// otherwise.\n612\t    pub fn wake_up(&mut self) -> Result<(), OsError> {\n613\t        unimplemented!()\n614\t    }\n615\t}\n616\t\n617\t// `delete_network_thread` is dropped from the Rust API:\n618\t// `Box<NetworkThreadCtx>` going out of scope signals shutdown,\n619\t// waits for the loop to exit, and frees the context (Drop in Phase 3).\n620\t\n621\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n622\t// Built-in thread hooks (platform defaults).\n623\t\n624\t/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadCreateFn`]\n625\t/// using the platform `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n626\t/// C: `int internal_thread_create(void**,\n627\t/// thread_fn, void*)`.\n628\t///\n629\t/// Returns `Ok(JoinHandle)` carrying the std handle, or\n630\t/// `Err(OsError)` with the platform errno.\n631\tpub fn internal_thread_create(\n632\t    thread_fn: Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + 'static>,\n633\t) -> Result<JoinHandle<()>, OsError> {\n634\t    Ok(std::thread::spawn(thread_fn))\n635\t}\n636\t\n637\t/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadDeleteFn`].\n638\t/// C: `void internal_thread_delete(void**)`.\n639\t///\n640\t/// In Rust this is a no-op — dropping the [`JoinHandle`] detaches\n641\t/// the thread; if the caller wants to wait for completion they\n642\t/// call `join()` themselves first.\n643\tpub fn internal_thread_delete(thread: JoinHandle<()>) {\n644\t    drop(thread);\n645\t}\n646\t\n647\t/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadSetnameFn`].\n648\t/// C: `void internal_thread_setname(char const*)`.\n649\t///\n650\t/// Thread naming requires OS-specific APIs not available in `std`;\n651\t/// this implementation is a no-op.  Concrete backends may override\n652\t/// via [`CustomThreadSetnameFn`].\n653\tpub fn internal_thread_setname(_thread_name: &str) {}\n654\t\n655\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n656\t// Quic: context helpers wired into the demo apps.\n657\t\n658\timpl Quic {\n659\t    /// Look up the network-thread context attached to this QUIC\n660\t    /// context (set by [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`] via\n661\t    /// `quic->v_thread_ctx`), or `None` if the QUIC context isn't\n662\t    /// driven by a packet-loop thread.\n663\t    /// C: `struct st_network_thread_ctx_t* get_thread_ctx(picoquic_quic_t*)`.\n664\t    pub fn thread_ctx(&mut self) -> Option<&mut NetworkThreadCtx> {\n665\t        let b = self.v_thread_ctx.as_mut()?;\n666\t        b.as_mut().downcast_mut::<NetworkThreadCtx>()\n667\t    }\n668\t\n669\t    /// Build a server-side QUIC context with the extra hooks\n670\t    /// (`alpn_select_fn`, key-log, qlog, perflog, LB-CID config) that\n671\t    /// the demo server installs after [`Config::create_and_configure`].\n672\t    /// C: `int server_set_context(picoquic_quic_t** qserver, …)`.\n673\t    ///\n674\t    /// The C `picoquic_quic_t** qserver` out-parameter folds into the\n675\t    /// `Ok(Box<Quic>)` payload.\n676\t    pub fn create_server(\n677\t        _config: &mut Config,\n678\t        _current_time: Instant,\n679\t        _default_callback: Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>,\n680\t        _alpn_select_fn: Option<Box<dyn AlpnSelect>>,\n681\t    ) -> Result<Box<Quic>, Error> {\n682\t        unimplemented!()\n683\t    }\n684\t}\n685\t\n686\timpl Config {\n687\t    /// Spawn `thread_ctxs.len()` server packet-loop threads, one\n688\t    /// [`Quic`] per thread, sharing the supplied callbacks.  Each\n689\t    /// QUIC context is created via [`Quic::create_server`].\n690\t    /// C: `int start_server_threads(…)`.\n691\t    ///\n692\t    /// The C `network_thread_ctx_t** thread_ctxs` out-array becomes\n693\t    /// a `&mut [Option<Box<…>>]` slice — the slot count carries the\n694\t    /// C `nb_threads_max`, successful threads land in the slots,\n695\t    /// and the number actually started is the `Ok` payload\n696\t    /// (replacing the C `int* nb_threads_created`).\n697\t    #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n698\t    pub fn start_server_threads(\n699\t        &mut self,\n700\t        _current_time: Instant,\n701\t        _alpn_select_fn: Option<Box<dyn AlpnSelect>>,\n702\t        _default_callback: Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>,\n703\t        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n704\t        _thread_create_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadCreateFn>>,\n705\t        _thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n706\t        _thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n707\t        _thread_ctxs: &mut [Option<Box<NetworkThreadCtx>>],\n708\t    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n709\t        unimplemented!()\n710\t    }\n711\t}\n712\t\n713\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n714\t// SocketCtx: per-socket operations (exposed for unit tests).\n715\t\n716\timpl<S: crate::socks::Socket> SocketCtx<S> {\n717\t    /// Close the socket and reset the `fd` slot to `None`.\n718\t    /// C: `void packet_loop_close_socket(socket_ctx_t*)`.\n719\t    /// Exposed directly so `sockloop_test.c`-derived tests can reach it.\n720\t    pub fn close(&mut self) {\n721\t        self.fd = None;\n722\t        self.is_started = false;\n723\t    }\n724\t}\n725\t\n726\t/// Open the per-thread socket pair(s) used by the loop.  C:\n727\t/// `int packet_loop_open_sockets(…, socket_ctx_t* s_ctx, …)`.\n728\t///\n729\t/// `s_ctx` must hold at least [`PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`] slots.\n730\t/// Returns the number of sockets actually opened; `Err` on a\n731\t/// non-recoverable open failure.\n732\t#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n733\tpub fn open_sockets<S: crate::socks::Socket>(\n734\t    _local_port: u16,\n735\t    _local_af: i32,\n736\t    _public_port: u16,\n737\t    _is_shared: bool,\n738\t    _socket_buffer_size: i32,\n739\t    _extra_socket_required: bool,\n740\t    _do_not_use_gso: bool,\n741\t    _s_ctx: &mut [SocketCtx<S>],\n742\t    _ecn_value: u8,\n743\t) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n744\t    unimplemented!()\n745\t}\n746\t\n747\t#[cfg(test)]\n748\tmod test {}\n749\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbc51140-68a0-4c62-b36a-79f6bbd7de93","uuid":"7d1387f7-1521-4c63-aa60-14950ba73373","timestamp":"2026-05-06T11:19:26.515Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/packet_loop.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/packet_loop.h`.\n//!\n//! Bundle of types and entry points implementing quic's\n//! reference event loop (`sockloop.c`) on top of the [`socks`]\n//! UDP shims.  The loop:\n//!\n//! * owns one or more [`SocketCtx`] per network family,\n//! * polls them with [`socks::select`] (or `select`/\n//!   `WSAWaitForMultipleEvents` in the C original),\n//! * routes received datagrams into quic, and\n//! * fires application callbacks ([`PacketLoopCbFn`]) at\n//!   well-defined points (loop ready, after recv, after send,\n//!   address change, time check, system-call duration spike, wake-up,\n//!   alt-port).\n//!\n//! Three layered entry points cover the same loop with different\n//! parameter shapes:\n//!\n//! * [`run`] — the original 1.x signature, preserved for\n//!   compatibility.  Builds a [`LoopParam`] on the stack and forwards.\n//! * [`run_v2`] — takes a [`LoopParam`] directly.\n//! * [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] — takes a fully-populated\n//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`]; the threaded entry point.\n//!\n//! Plus the threading helpers ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`] et al.)\n//! that wrap `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n//!\n//! Phase 4: threading entry points (`run_v2`, `run`, spawn family,\n//! `open_sockets`) are `unimplemented!()` — threading is deferred to\n//! v2 per TRANSLATE_PLAN.md.  Leaf helpers are implemented.\n//!\n//! [`socks`]: crate::socks\n//!\n//! ## Pointer-shape decisions\n//!\n//! Read from `quic/sockloop.c`, `quic/winsockloop.c`, and\n//! the demo callers in `first/demo.c`,\n//! `sample/sample_*.c`, and `test/sockloop_test.c`:\n//!\n//! * `Quic*` parameters are non-null in every observed\n//!   caller — they translate to `&mut Quic`.\n//! * `LoopParam*` is *borrowed* by the loop\n//!   helpers (the caller stack-allocates it in\n//!   `run_v2` / the demo apps) — `&mut` here.  Inside\n//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`] the same pointer is *owned*\n//!   when `is_param_allocated == 1` ([`Config::start_server_threads`]\n//!   `malloc`s a copy and `delete_network_thread` `free`s\n//!   it).  Phase 1 keeps the field as `Option<Box<…>>` and folds\n//!   the C `is_param_allocated` flag away — the `Box` itself\n//!   carries ownership; borrowed-param call sites store `None`\n//!   plus a separate borrow at runtime.\n//! * `picoquic_quic_config_t*` is borrowed-mut by\n//!   [`Quic::create_server`] and [`Config::start_server_threads`]\n//!   (they read and may mutate fields like `ticket_encryption_key`).\n//! * Function-pointer typedefs (`packet_loop_cb_fn`,\n//!   `custom_thread_create_fn`, `_setname_fn`,\n//!   `_delete_fn`) become traits per the Phase 1 rules; the C `void*\n//!   callback_ctx` companion folds into the trait implementor's\n//!   state.  The C `(cb_mode, void* callback_argv)` tagged-union\n//!   pair becomes the typed [`LoopEvent`] enum — each variant\n//!   carries the payload type the C `cb_mode` implied.\n//! * `SocketCtx* s_ctx` is used as both a single object\n//!   ([`SocketCtx::close`]) and a fixed-size array\n//!   ([`open_sockets`] writes up to `PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`\n//!   entries).  The single-object path is a method on [`SocketCtx`];\n//!   the array path takes `&mut [SocketCtx]`, with the slice length\n//!   subsuming the C convention of \"callee writes and returns the count\".\n//! * `NetworkThreadCtx**` outputs from\n//!   [`Config::start_server_threads`] become `&mut [Option<Box<…>>]`\n//!   — the slice carries `nb_threads_max`; each slot is filled with\n//!   `Some` on success.\n//! * Bitfields collapse to `bool`s.  Single-bit C `int : 1` /\n//!   `unsigned int : 1` flag fields don't justify `bitflags!` on a\n//!   set of unrelated booleans (see `config.rs` for the\n//!   established convention).\n//! * `volatile int` fields in [`NetworkThreadCtx`]\n//!   become plain `i32` / `bool` — `Send`/`Sync` is out of v1 scope,\n//!   so the volatile semantics have nowhere to land.\n//! * `sockaddr_storage` fields fold into `Option<SocketAddr>`,\n//!   matching the [`socks`] convention (`AF_UNSPEC` ↔ `None`).\n//! * The Windows-only fields (`overlap`, `WSARecvMsg`, `WSASendMsg`,\n//!   `dataBuf`, `msg`, …) and the io_uring fields are dropped per\n//!   the v1 single-target scope.\n//! * `recv_buffer: uint8_t* + recv_buffer_size: size_t` is an\n//!   owning pair (allocated in `packet_set_windows_socket`\n//!   on the Windows path, or `packet_loop_recv_buf_uring_init`\n//!   on io_uring; freed in [`SocketCtx::close`]).\n//!   On the canonical Linux/`select` build neither is used — every\n//!   datagram lands in the shared loop buffer.  The field shape is\n//!   `Option<Vec<u8>>` so the unused state is `None`; Vec subsumes\n//!   the (pointer, size) pair into one owning value.\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse std::thread::JoinHandle;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::Instant;\nuse crate::config::Config;\nuse crate::socks::OsError;\nuse crate::{AlpnSelect, Quic, StreamDataCallback};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Compile-time limits.\n\n/// Maximum number of UDP sockets a single packet loop can drive at\n/// once (one per address family, optionally times two for the\n/// public/private port split).  C: `PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`.\npub const PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX: usize = 4;\n\n/// Maximum datagrams pulled from one socket per loop iteration.\n/// C: `PACKET_LOOP_RECV_MAX`.\npub const PACKET_LOOP_RECV_MAX: usize = 10;\n\n/// Maximum datagrams pushed to one socket per loop iteration.\n/// C: `PACKET_LOOP_SEND_MAX`.\npub const PACKET_LOOP_SEND_MAX: usize = 10;\n\n/// Hard cap on how long the loop will wait between iterations\n/// (microseconds).  C: `PACKET_LOOP_SEND_DELAY_MAX`.\npub const PACKET_LOOP_SEND_DELAY_MAX: u64 = 2500;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Per-socket context.\n\n/// State the loop maintains per UDP socket.  C: `socket_ctx_t`.\n///\n/// Trimmed to the canonical Linux build: the Windows\n/// (`WSAOVERLAPPED`, `WSARecvMsg`, …) and io_uring (`msghdr`,\n/// `iovec`, `ctrl_buffer`) fields are dropped per the v1\n/// single-target scope.  The four C bitfields collapse to plain\n/// `bool` flags.\npub struct SocketCtx<S: crate::socks::Socket> {\n    /// OS socket handle.  `None` mirrors the C `INVALID_SOCKET`\n    /// sentinel for \"not yet open\".  Generic over the active\n    /// `Socket` implementation; the default in production is\n    /// `crate::socks_socket2::Socket2Udp` (wraps `socket2::Socket`).\n    pub fd: Option<S>,\n    /// Address family the socket was opened in (`AF_INET`,\n    /// `AF_INET6`).  Stays `i32` to match call sites that pass the\n    /// libc `AF_*` constants directly.\n    pub af: i32,\n    /// Port number the socket is bound to (host byte order).\n    pub port: u16,\n    /// `htons(port)` cached so the loop can stamp it into received\n    /// destination addresses without a per-packet byte-swap.\n    pub n_port: u16,\n    /// Private port number — the port reserved for this thread when\n    /// the public port is shared with siblings (see\n    /// `is_port_shared`).  C: `private_port`.\n    pub private_port: u16,\n    /// Whether this socket is bound to a port shared with other\n    /// threads (e.g., port 443 across an H3 thread pool — uses\n    /// `SO_REUSEPORT`).  C: `int is_port_shared : 1`.\n    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n    /// Whether [`open_sockets`]-equivalent setup has completed\n    /// for this socket.  C: `unsigned int is_started : 1`.\n    pub is_started: bool,\n    /// Whether the kernel honored `UDP_SEGMENT` (GSO) on this\n    /// socket.  C: `unsigned int supports_udp_send_coalesced : 1`.\n    pub supports_udp_send_coalesced: bool,\n    /// Whether the kernel honored `UDP_GRO` on this socket.\n    /// C: `unsigned int supports_udp_recv_coalesced : 1`.\n    pub supports_udp_recv_coalesced: bool,\n    /// Owning receive buffer.  Used only on the Windows-async and\n    /// io_uring paths; the canonical Linux build leaves it as\n    /// `None` and uses the shared loop buffer instead.\n    /// C: `uint8_t* recv_buffer` plus the `recv_buffer_size`\n    /// companion (length is implicit in the slice).\n    pub recv_buffer: Option<Box<[u8]>>,\n    /// Source address of the most recent datagram (`recvmsg`'s\n    /// `msg_name`).  C: `struct sockaddr_storage addr_from` plus\n    /// `from_length` — `Option<SocketAddr>` carries both.\n    pub addr_from: Option<SocketAddr>,\n    /// Destination address parsed out of `IP_PKTINFO` /\n    /// `IPV6_PKTINFO` for the most recent datagram.\n    /// C: `struct sockaddr_storage addr_dest` + `dest_length`.\n    pub addr_dest: Option<SocketAddr>,\n    /// Receiving interface index (from the same cmsg).\n    /// C: `int dest_if`.\n    pub dest_if: i32,\n    /// `IP_TOS` / `IPV6_TCLASS` ECN code-point on the most recent\n    /// datagram.  C: `unsigned char received_ecn`.\n    pub received_ecn: u8,\n    /// Number of bytes read into the loop buffer on the most recent\n    /// recv.  C: `int bytes_recv` (C uses `-1` for error; the Rust\n    /// loop propagates errors through `Result` at the call site and\n    /// only writes a valid byte count here).\n    pub bytes_recv: usize,\n    /// Scratch space for assembling outbound `cmsg` payloads\n    /// (`IP_PKTINFO`, `IPV6_PKTINFO`, `UDP_SEGMENT`).\n    /// C: `char cmsg_buffer[1024]`.\n    pub cmsg_buffer: [u8; 1024],\n    /// `UDP_GRO` segment size on the most recent datagram (the\n    /// kernel's `UDP_GRO` cmsg).  C: `size_t udp_coalesced_size`.\n    pub udp_coalesced_size: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<S: crate::socks::Socket> Default for SocketCtx<S> {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        SocketCtx {\n            fd: None,\n            af: 0,\n            port: 0,\n            n_port: 0,\n            private_port: 0,\n            is_port_shared: false,\n            is_started: false,\n            supports_udp_send_coalesced: false,\n            supports_udp_recv_coalesced: false,\n            recv_buffer: None,\n            addr_from: None,\n            addr_dest: None,\n            dest_if: 0,\n            received_ecn: 0,\n            bytes_recv: 0,\n            cmsg_buffer: [0; 1024],\n            udp_coalesced_size: 0,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Callback enum + companion arg structs.\n\n/// Loop-callback event delivered to [`PacketLoopCbFn::callback`].\n/// Replaces the C `(packet_loop_cb_enum cb_mode, void* callback_argv)`\n/// pair: the enum carries the per-event payload directly, so\n/// implementors no longer cast a `void*`.\n/// C: `packet_loop_cb_enum`.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub enum LoopEvent<'a> {\n    /// Loop has finished initializing.  C: `packet_loop_ready`,\n    /// `callback_argv: *mut LoopOptions`.\n    Ready(&'a mut LoopOptions),\n    /// Number of packets received this iteration.  C:\n    /// `packet_loop_after_receive`, `callback_argv: *mut size_t`.\n    AfterReceive(usize),\n    /// Number of packets sent this iteration.  C:\n    /// `packet_loop_after_send`, `callback_argv: *mut size_t`.\n    AfterSend(usize),\n    /// New local address advertised by the application after a\n    /// port update.  C: `packet_loop_port_update`,\n    /// `callback_argv: *mut sockaddr`.\n    PortUpdate(core::net::SocketAddr),\n    /// Optional check-in fired when the application set\n    /// [`LoopOptions::do_time_check`].  C:\n    /// `packet_loop_time_check`.\n    TimeCheck(&'a mut TimeCheckArg),\n    /// Optional system-call duration report fired when the\n    /// application set [`LoopOptions::do_system_call_duration`].\n    /// C: `packet_loop_system_call_duration`.\n    SystemCallDuration(&'a mut SystemCallDuration),\n    /// Wake-up event triggered by [`NetworkThreadCtx::wake_up`].\n    /// C: `packet_loop_wake_up` with `callback_argv: NULL`.\n    WakeUp,\n    /// Alt-port address surfaced for multipath / migration tests.\n    /// C: `packet_loop_alt_port`.\n    AltPort(core::net::SocketAddr),\n}\n\n/// System-call duration statistics surfaced through the optional\n/// [`LoopEvent::SystemCallDuration`] callback.\n/// C: `packet_loop_system_call_duration_t`.\n#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\npub struct SystemCallDuration {\n    /// Duration of the most recent zero-delay system call\n    /// (microseconds).\n    pub scd_last: u64,\n    /// Maximum observed system-call duration so far.\n    pub scd_max: u64,\n    /// Smoothed system-call duration (RFC-6298–style EWMA).\n    pub scd_smoothed: u64,\n    /// Mean absolute deviation around `scd_smoothed`.\n    pub scd_dev: u64,\n}\n\n/// In/out argument for the [`LoopEvent::TimeCheck`] callback.\n/// The loop fills `current_time` and a proposed `delta_t`;\n/// the application overwrites `delta_t` with a (possibly smaller)\n/// value if it has work to do sooner.\n/// C: `packet_loop_time_check_arg_t`.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]\npub struct TimeCheckArg {\n    /// Loop-time timestamp.\n    pub current_time: Instant,\n    /// Proposed sleep, in microseconds — application may shrink.\n    pub delta_t: i64,\n}\n\nimpl Default for TimeCheckArg {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        Self {\n            current_time: Instant::from_ticks(0),\n            delta_t: 0,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Application callback fired at the lifecycle and per-iteration\n/// events listed in [`LoopEvent`].\n///\n/// C: `int (*packet_loop_cb_fn)(picoquic_quic_t* quic,\n/// packet_loop_cb_enum cb_mode, void* callback_ctx, void*\n/// callback_argv)`.  The `callback_ctx` companion folds into the\n/// trait implementor's state; the `(cb_mode, callback_argv)` tagged\n/// union folds into [`LoopEvent`].\n///\n/// Returns `Ok(())` on success, or an error to break the loop.\npub trait PacketLoopCbFn {\n    fn callback(&mut self, quic: &mut Quic, event: LoopEvent<'_>) -> Result<(), Error>;\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Loop options + parameters.\n\n/// Feature-flags the application advertises in response to the\n/// [`LoopEvent::Ready`]\n/// callback.  C: `packet_loop_options_t`, three single-bit\n/// `unsigned int : 1` fields collapsed to `bool` per the\n/// established convention (see `config.rs`).\n#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\npub struct LoopOptions {\n    /// Application wants the loop to call back with\n    /// [`LoopEvent::TimeCheck`]\n    /// before each select.\n    pub do_time_check: bool,\n    /// Application wants notifications when zero-delay system calls\n    /// take noticeably long.\n    pub do_system_call_duration: bool,\n    /// Application provides an alternate port for migration /\n    /// multipath testing.\n    pub provide_alt_port: bool,\n}\n\n/// Loop-construction parameters (v2 / v3 entry points).\n/// C: `packet_loop_param_t`.  `repr(C)` is dropped — the\n/// struct never crosses an external boundary.\n#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\npub struct LoopParam {\n    /// Default port for outgoing connections.  `0` ⇒ ephemeral.\n    pub local_port: u16,\n    /// `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6`, or `0` for \"both\".\n    pub local_af: i32,\n    /// Outbound interface index (or `0` to leave it to the kernel).\n    pub dest_if: i32,\n    /// Server / public-facing port.  `0` for client-only loops.\n    pub public_port: u16,\n    /// Whether the public port is shared across this thread pool\n    /// (uses `SO_REUSEPORT`).\n    pub is_port_shared: bool,\n    /// `SO_SNDBUF` / `SO_RCVBUF` size, or `0` to skip the syscall.\n    pub socket_buffer_size: i32,\n    /// Disable `UDP_SEGMENT` GSO even if the kernel advertises it.\n    pub do_not_use_gso: bool,\n    /// Open the public-port socket pair too (needed for migration).\n    pub extra_socket_required: bool,\n    /// When both port pairs exist, prefer the extra socket as the\n    /// outgoing source.\n    pub prefer_extra_socket: bool,\n    /// Inject a synthetic `EIO` once into the send path (debug\n    /// hook).\n    pub simulate_eio: bool,\n    /// Cap on the size of an individual coalesced send.  `0` ⇒\n    /// kernel default.\n    pub send_length_max: usize,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Custom thread-management hooks.\n\n/// Spawn-a-thread hook.  C:\n/// `int (*custom_thread_create_fn)(void** thread_id,\n/// thread_fn thread_fn, void* arg)`.\n///\n/// In C, `(thread_fn, arg)` is the trampoline + state pair the new\n/// thread will run.  In Rust, [`std::thread::spawn`] already takes\n/// an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send` closure (which subsumes the C\n/// \"function pointer + void* arg\" pattern), so the hook reduces to\n/// \"produce a [`JoinHandle`] from that closure\".\npub trait CustomThreadCreateFn {\n    /// Create a thread that runs `thread_fn` to completion.  The\n    /// returned [`JoinHandle`] is owned by the caller and passed\n    /// back through [`CustomThreadDeleteFn::delete`] at teardown.\n    ///\n    /// The C contract returns `0` for success / non-zero `errno`\n    /// otherwise; the [`OsError`] payload carries the same value\n    /// that the C `*ret` out-parameter would on\n    /// [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`].\n    fn create(\n        &mut self,\n        thread_fn: Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + 'static>,\n    ) -> Result<JoinHandle<()>, OsError>;\n}\n\n/// Set-thread-name hook.  C:\n/// `void (*custom_thread_setname_fn)(char const* thread_name)`.\n///\n/// Length is capped at 16 bytes (including the trailing NUL) on\n/// Linux's `prctl(PR_SET_NAME, …)`; the trait takes a `&str` slice\n/// and lets the implementor truncate / re-encode as needed.\npub trait CustomThreadSetnameFn {\n    /// Apply `thread_name` to the *current* thread.  Called from\n    /// inside the thread after it starts, per the C convention.\n    fn set_name(&mut self, thread_name: &str);\n}\n\n/// Tear-down-a-thread hook.  C:\n/// `void (*custom_thread_delete_fn)(void** thread_id)`.\n///\n/// In Rust the standard `JoinHandle` cleans itself up on drop, so\n/// this hook only matters for backends that allocate their own\n/// out-of-band thread bookkeeping; the default\n/// [`internal_thread_delete`] just lets the handle go.\npub trait CustomThreadDeleteFn {\n    /// Release any resources tied to `thread`.  Called from the\n    /// destruction path of [`NetworkThreadCtx`].\n    fn delete(&mut self, thread: JoinHandle<()>);\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Threaded packet-loop context.\n\n/// Thread context carrying everything the v3 entry point needs to\n/// run a packet loop.  C: `network_thread_ctx_t`.\n///\n/// Two ownership modes coexist in C:\n///\n/// * Foreground / blocking (`run_v2`): the caller stack-allocates a\n///   `NetworkThreadCtx` and calls [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] on it.\n///   `is_threaded` stays `false`, no wake-up plumbing is created.\n/// * Background ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`]): the helper\n///   allocates the context, populates it, opens the wake-up\n///   pipe / event, and launches the OS thread; teardown via\n///   `Drop` (Phase 3) frees everything.\n///\n/// Phase 1 keeps a single struct shape with optional fields so both\n/// modes fit; Phase 3 may split them or introduce a builder.\n///\n/// `volatile` qualifiers in the C source are dropped — `Send`/`Sync`\n/// is out of v1 scope and the thread-shutdown handshake uses these\n/// flags through the same single-threaded code path the rest of the\n/// crate assumes.\npub struct NetworkThreadCtx {\n    // The C field `picoquic_quic_t* quic` is gone.  v1 is\n    // single-threaded (per `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`), so the loop's\n    // entry points (`Quic::run_loop` etc.) take `&mut Quic`\n    // explicitly rather than threading a back-pointer through\n    // this struct.  v2 will reintroduce a typed cross-thread\n    // handle (`Arc<Mutex<Quic>>` or whatever the threading model\n    // ends up using); the placeholder here is intentionally\n    // absent.\n    /// Loop parameters, optionally owned (`is_param_allocated`).\n    /// Phase 1 collapses the C borrowed-or-owned discriminant into\n    /// a single `Option<Box<…>>`; the borrowed case stores `None`\n    /// and the borrow lives outside this struct.\n    pub param: Option<Box<LoopParam>>,\n    /// Application loop callback.  `None` matches the C `NULL`\n    /// (the loop runs without notifying the app on each event —\n    /// used by the bench harness).\n    pub loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n    /// Custom thread-deletion hook; defaults to\n    /// `internal_thread_delete` when the C caller passes\n    /// `NULL`.\n    pub thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n    /// Custom thread-naming hook; defaults to\n    /// `internal_thread_setname`.\n    pub thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n    /// Optional thread name.  Capped at 16 bytes (including NUL)\n    /// on Linux per `prctl(PR_SET_NAME, …)`.\n    pub thread_name: Option<String>,\n    /// Underlying OS thread handle.  `None` for the foreground v2\n    /// entry path (no thread is spawned).\n    pub pthread: Option<JoinHandle<()>>,\n    /// Wake-up pipe (Linux: `pipe2`).  `[ -1, -1 ]` when no wake-up\n    /// has been opened (`wake_up_defined == false`).  The Windows\n    /// `HANDLE wake_up_event` variant is dropped per the v1 scope.\n    pub wake_up_pipe_fd: [i32; 2],\n    /// Whether [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`] actually\n    /// spawned a thread (vs. the foreground path that reuses the\n    /// caller's stack).  C: `int is_threaded`.\n    pub is_threaded: bool,\n    /// Whether [`Self::wake_up_pipe_fd`] was successfully opened.\n    /// C: `int wake_up_defined`.\n    pub wake_up_defined: bool,\n    /// Set by the loop once it finishes initialization (after the\n    /// first [`LoopEvent::Ready`] callback).\n    /// C: `volatile int thread_is_ready`.\n    pub thread_is_ready: bool,\n    /// Set from the outside to ask the loop to exit (the `Drop`\n    /// implementation will set this in Phase 3).\n    /// C: `volatile int thread_should_close`.\n    pub thread_should_close: bool,\n    /// Set by the loop on the way out, before returning from v3.\n    /// C: `volatile int thread_is_closed`.\n    pub thread_is_closed: bool,\n    /// Final return code from the loop body.  Read by the caller\n    /// of [`run_v2`] after [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] returns.\n    /// C: `int return_code`.\n    pub return_code: i32,\n}\n\nimpl Default for NetworkThreadCtx {\n    fn default() -> Self {\n        NetworkThreadCtx {\n            param: None,\n            loop_callback: None,\n            thread_delete_fn: None,\n            thread_setname_fn: None,\n            thread_name: None,\n            pthread: None,\n            wake_up_pipe_fd: [-1, -1],\n            is_threaded: false,\n            wake_up_defined: false,\n            thread_is_ready: false,\n            thread_should_close: false,\n            thread_is_closed: false,\n            return_code: 0,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Loop entry points.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Drive the packet loop until the application or an error breaks\n    /// it.  C: `int packet_loop_v2(…)`.\n    ///\n    /// Builds a transient [`NetworkThreadCtx`] on the stack and calls\n    /// [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`].  The return code is what `run` stored\n    /// in `thread_ctx.return_code`.\n    pub fn run_v2(\n        &mut self,\n        _param: &mut LoopParam,\n        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Legacy single-call entry point; builds a [`LoopParam`] from\n    /// positional arguments and forwards to [`run_v2`].\n    /// C: `int packet_loop(…)`.\n    pub fn run(\n        &mut self,\n        _local_port: i32,\n        _local_af: i32,\n        _dest_if: i32,\n        _socket_buffer_size: i32,\n        _do_not_use_gso: bool,\n        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// NetworkThreadCtx: background-thread management and the v3 entry point.\n\nimpl NetworkThreadCtx {\n    /// Run the packet loop using `self` as the fully-populated thread\n    /// context.  C: `void* packet_loop_v3(void* v_ctx)`.\n    ///\n    /// The C entry returns its `void*` exit code only to satisfy the\n    /// thread-function prototype; callers read the result back from\n    /// `return_code`.\n    pub fn run(&mut self) {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Spawn a packet loop on its own OS thread using the platform\n    /// default (`pthread_create` / `CreateThread`).\n    /// C: `network_thread_ctx_t* start_network_thread(…)`.\n    ///\n    /// Returns `Ok(Box<Self>)` on success — the box owns the\n    /// heap-allocated context.  `Err(OsError)` carries the OS errno\n    /// from the wake-up pipe or thread-create syscall (the C `*ret`\n    /// out-parameter folds into the result).\n    pub fn spawn(\n        _quic: &mut Quic,\n        _param: LoopParam,\n        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n    ) -> Result<Box<Self>, OsError> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Spawn the packet loop using application-supplied thread hooks.\n    /// `None` for any hook selects the platform default — same\n    /// semantics as the C `NULL` argument.\n    /// C: `network_thread_ctx_t* start_custom_network_thread(…)`.\n    #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n    pub fn spawn_custom(\n        _quic: &mut Quic,\n        _param: LoopParam,\n        _thread_create_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadCreateFn>>,\n        _thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n        _thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n        _thread_name: Option<&str>,\n        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n    ) -> Result<Box<Self>, OsError> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n\n    /// Write to the wake-up pipe so the loop's next iteration runs\n    /// immediately and fires [`LoopEvent::WakeUp`].\n    /// C: `int wake_up_network_thread(network_thread_ctx_t*)`.\n    ///\n    /// Returns `Err(OsError)` with the OS errno on failure, `Ok(())`\n    /// otherwise.\n    pub fn wake_up(&mut self) -> Result<(), OsError> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// `delete_network_thread` is dropped from the Rust API:\n// `Box<NetworkThreadCtx>` going out of scope signals shutdown,\n// waits for the loop to exit, and frees the context (Drop in Phase 3).\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Built-in thread hooks (platform defaults).\n\n/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadCreateFn`]\n/// using the platform `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n/// C: `int internal_thread_create(void**,\n/// thread_fn, void*)`.\n///\n/// Returns `Ok(JoinHandle)` carrying the std handle, or\n/// `Err(OsError)` with the platform errno.\npub fn internal_thread_create(\n    thread_fn: Box<dyn FnOnce() + Send + 'static>,\n) -> Result<JoinHandle<()>, OsError> {\n    Ok(std::thread::spawn(thread_fn))\n}\n\n/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadDeleteFn`].\n/// C: `void internal_thread_delete(void**)`.\n///\n/// In Rust this is a no-op — dropping the [`JoinHandle`] detaches\n/// the thread; if the caller wants to wait for completion they\n/// call `join()` themselves first.\npub fn internal_thread_delete(thread: JoinHandle<()>) {\n    drop(thread);\n}\n\n/// Default implementation of [`CustomThreadSetnameFn`].\n/// C: `void internal_thread_setname(char const*)`.\n///\n/// Thread naming requires OS-specific APIs not available in `std`;\n/// this implementation is a no-op.  Concrete backends may override\n/// via [`CustomThreadSetnameFn`].\npub fn internal_thread_setname(_thread_name: &str) {}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Quic: context helpers wired into the demo apps.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Look up the network-thread context attached to this QUIC\n    /// context (set by [`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn_custom`] via\n    /// `quic->v_thread_ctx`), or `None` if the QUIC context isn't\n    /// driven by a packet-loop thread.\n    /// C: `struct st_network_thread_ctx_t* get_thread_ctx(picoquic_quic_t*)`.\n    pub fn thread_ctx(&mut self) -> Option<&mut NetworkThreadCtx> {\n        let b = self.v_thread_ctx.as_mut()?;\n        b.as_mut().downcast_mut::<NetworkThreadCtx>()\n    }\n\n    /// Build a server-side QUIC context with the extra hooks\n    /// (`alpn_select_fn`, key-log, qlog, perflog, LB-CID config) that\n    /// the demo server installs after [`Config::create_and_configure`].\n    /// C: `int server_set_context(picoquic_quic_t** qserver, …)`.\n    ///\n    /// The C `picoquic_quic_t** qserver` out-parameter folds into the\n    /// `Ok(Box<Quic>)` payload.\n    pub fn create_server(\n        _config: &mut Config,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _default_callback: Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>,\n        _alpn_select_fn: Option<Box<dyn AlpnSelect>>,\n    ) -> Result<Box<Quic>, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Config {\n    /// Spawn `thread_ctxs.len()` server packet-loop threads, one\n    /// [`Quic`] per thread, sharing the supplied callbacks.  Each\n    /// QUIC context is created via [`Quic::create_server`].\n    /// C: `int start_server_threads(…)`.\n    ///\n    /// The C `network_thread_ctx_t** thread_ctxs` out-array becomes\n    /// a `&mut [Option<Box<…>>]` slice — the slot count carries the\n    /// C `nb_threads_max`, successful threads land in the slots,\n    /// and the number actually started is the `Ok` payload\n    /// (replacing the C `int* nb_threads_created`).\n    #[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n    pub fn start_server_threads(\n        &mut self,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _alpn_select_fn: Option<Box<dyn AlpnSelect>>,\n        _default_callback: Option<Box<dyn StreamDataCallback>>,\n        _loop_callback: Option<Box<dyn PacketLoopCbFn>>,\n        _thread_create_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadCreateFn>>,\n        _thread_delete_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadDeleteFn>>,\n        _thread_setname_fn: Option<Box<dyn CustomThreadSetnameFn>>,\n        _thread_ctxs: &mut [Option<Box<NetworkThreadCtx>>],\n    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n        unimplemented!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// SocketCtx: per-socket operations (exposed for unit tests).\n\nimpl<S: crate::socks::Socket> SocketCtx<S> {\n    /// Close the socket and reset the `fd` slot to `None`.\n    /// C: `void packet_loop_close_socket(socket_ctx_t*)`.\n    /// Exposed directly so `sockloop_test.c`-derived tests can reach it.\n    pub fn close(&mut self) {\n        self.fd = None;\n        self.is_started = false;\n    }\n}\n\n/// Open the per-thread socket pair(s) used by the loop.  C:\n/// `int packet_loop_open_sockets(…, socket_ctx_t* s_ctx, …)`.\n///\n/// `s_ctx` must hold at least [`PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`] slots.\n/// Returns the number of sockets actually opened; `Err` on a\n/// non-recoverable open failure.\n#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\npub fn open_sockets<S: crate::socks::Socket>(\n    _local_port: u16,\n    _local_af: i32,\n    _public_port: u16,\n    _is_shared: bool,\n    _socket_buffer_size: i32,\n    _extra_socket_required: bool,\n    _do_not_use_gso: bool,\n    _s_ctx: &mut [SocketCtx<S>],\n    _ecn_value: u8,\n) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n    unimplemented!()\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod test {}\n","numLines":749,"startLine":1,"totalLines":749}}}
